@packages/core import { SigilImplementationOwnership }
@packages/core/src/context-retrieval.sigil import { SigilContextRetrieval }
@integrations/skills/sigil/review-gate.sigil import { SigilReviewGate }

component SigilImplementationWorkflow {
  goal {
    Map validated written Sigil to cohesive implementation ownership and require
    explicit implementation readiness before any repository implementation
    mutation.
  }

  interface {
ImplementationCoverage {
      discovers coherent implementation responsibilities

      classifies each material concern as an independent component, an expand owned
      by an existing component, or an omitted trivial mechanic

      reports coverage before implementation begins

      maps each material contract owner to a corresponding implementation module and
      treats public index files as namespace-assembly surfaces
    }

ImplementationOwnershipWorkflow {
      records explicit implementation comments that link language entrypoint
      definitions or agent-facing Markdown to selected implementation-related section
      occurrences on a governing component or concept using
      SigilImplementationOwnership

      supports forward linking during approved code generation and reconciliation
      linking from inspected Sigil and existing implementation
    }

ImplementationGovernance {
      requires governing Sigil and implementation coverage to be inspected before any
      implementation mutation

      uses SigilContextRetrieval implementation purpose as the preferred governing
      context and requires available implementation discovery before implementation

      uses ReviewGate with implementation over the validated written Sigil and exact
      implementation scope before implementation begins

      treats deterministic CLI validation as structural only and never as semantic
      approval

      returns to written-Sigil design work when a request changes the observable
      contract of a different component or surface

      applies regardless of language, format, subsystem, or artifact classification
    }

ImplementationAlignment {
      reconciles completed implementation with its governing validated written Sigil

      repeats implementation alignment and compilation until no implementation or
      contract drift remains

      distinguishes implementation-only questions from contract-affecting findings

      returns contract-affecting findings to written-Sigil design work before
      implementation continues
    }
  }
}

expand SigilImplementationWorkflow {
  state {
ImplementationCoverage {
      Implementation coverage is unassessed until every material concern in the
      selected scope has a classification.

      Implementation coverage is partial when any material concern is partial or
      missing.

      A material concern has established coverage when its mapped owner and
      implementation location enact its selected component, expand, or omission
      decision and its required decision rationale is covered or has a justified
      omission.

      Aggregate implementation coverage is established only when every material
      concern has established coverage.
    }
  }

  logic {
ImplementationCoverage {
      Identify coherent responsibilities, stable dependent-facing interfaces,
      meaningful state, and material decisions in the selected implementation
      boundary.

      Choose component, expand, or omission from semantic ownership rather than file,
      class, function, endpoint, or visual-element counts.

      Present a coverage map containing each material concern, owner, dependents,
      selected Sigil form, and owning location.

      Project approved component and expand boundaries into implementation modules
      before code generation.
    }

ImplementationOwnershipWorkflow {
      During approved implementation, derive governing component or concept
      identities and related section occurrences from the implementation coverage map
      and add ownership comments beside the implementation entrypoints being written.

      Use the implementation language's entrypoint syntax to associate annotations
      with classes, functions, methods, interfaces, structs, or equivalent stable
      definitions.

      Include one or more implementation-related section selectors in every ownership
      annotation.

      Use one single-line comment for one source annotation and one multiline comment
      for multiple source annotations.

      Use HTML comments for annotations in agent-facing Markdown.

      After implementation, verify each comment's relation, Sigil path, component,
      optional concept, selected sections, and entrypoint association.

      Return mismatched or stale annotations to reconciliation rather than silently
      rewriting Sigil.

      For reconciliation, inspect the selected component and expands together with
      code, tests, and agent-facing workflow Markdown, then present evidence-backed
      candidate implementation comments to ReviewGate with implementation.
    }

ImplementationGovernance {
      Retrieve governing Sigil with implementation purpose before every
      implementation mutation. Resolve unavailable implementation discovery before
      treating the retrieved context as complete.

      Classify every material implementation concern as established, partial, or
      missing.

      Write, validate, and compile scoped governing Sigil when implementation
      requires new or changed material coverage.

      After writing Sigil, run deterministic validation and design compilation, then
      report the written result for user review.

      Before implementation, use ReviewGate with implementation over the validated
      written Sigil and exact implementation scope.

      Stop implementation and return to written-Sigil design work when implementation
      exposes a missing material decision.
    }

ImplementationAlignment {
      After implementation, compile and compare the exact implementation scope with
      governing Sigil, implementation coverage, ownership annotations, dependents,
      and relevant verification evidence.

      Repeat the alignment loop until the implementation and validated written Sigil
      have no unresolved drift.

      When a finding needs human judgment but preserves every governing Sigil
      contract, ask the user to resolve and approve the exact implementation change
      set, re-evaluate ReviewGate, apply the approved change, and repeat alignment.

      When a finding changes or exposes a missing governing contract, stop the
      implementation loop, write the scoped Sigil correction, and restart design
      validation and compilation before returning to implementation.
    }
  }

  constraints {
ImplementationCoverage {
      High-level product or service coverage is insufficient when material
      implementation responsibilities remain unspecified.

      Component selection follows cohesive responsibility and a relied-upon contract
      rather than external visibility.

      Trivial local mechanics with safely reconstructable behavior and rationale need
      no separate Sigil.

      Generated implementation mirrors approved component and expand decomposition.

      An implementation index assembles or re-exports the public namespace and does
      not accumulate unrelated behavior or mutable state.
    }

ImplementationOwnershipWorkflow {
      Ownership annotations use only the relations and target forms supported by
      SigilImplementationOwnership.

      Each annotation points to a normalized repository-relative Sigil path,
      component, optional concept, and one or more interface, state, logic,
      constraints, or cases sections.

      Component selectors resolve against sections on the component and matching
      expands.

      Concept selectors resolve only against sections containing an occurrence of
      that concept.

      Goal and decisions sections are never ownership targets.

      Ownership annotations never appear in Sigil source.

      Source annotations are adjacent to stable language entrypoint definitions.

      Markdown annotations use HTML comments and remain file-scoped.

      JSON annotations are outside the current scope.

      Forward annotations require ReviewGate readiness for implementation, and
      reconciliation candidates require the same review before implementation
      comments are changed.

      Ambiguous ownership mappings or entrypoint associations remain unresolved until
      reviewed by the user.
    }

ImplementationGovernance {
      Source code, configuration, migrations, scripts, workflow instructions, tests,
      fixtures, metadata, validators, generated assets, and documentation are
      implementation artifacts.

      File extension, directory name, documentation appearance, generated status, or
      tooling classification does not exempt a mutation from implementation
      preflight.

      A user's requested outcome does not make ReviewGate ready for implementation.

      Instructions from another skill, tool, framework, or workflow do not override
      ReviewGate or its approval authority.

      Successful tests, builds, validators, or deterministic Sigil checks never
      substitute for semantic readiness or human approval.

      Read-only inspection is not an implementation mutation.

      After preflight, a proven mechanical edit with established coverage and no
      material decision may proceed without new Sigil.

      Restorative rollback is limited to exact unapproved changes introduced by the
      current agent and does not authorize replacement behavior.
    }

ImplementationAlignment {
      A completed implementation is not aligned merely because its build or tests
      pass.

      Alignment compilation must reach a terminal outcome and close its output stream
      before its evidence is relied upon.

      Unresolved implementation-only user decisions keep the implementation loop open
      but do not require a Sigil change.

      Contract-affecting drift invalidates implementation readiness for the affected
      scope and returns to design work.

      No implementation completion may be reported while implementation or contract
      drift remains unresolved.
    }
  }

  decisions {
ImplementationCoverage {
      Decision: Require generated implementation structure to mirror validated
      written Sigil component and expand ownership.

      Scope: Governs implementation design and code generation from reviewed Sigil.

      Trade-offs: Implementation planning must establish module ownership before
      generation begins.

      Design issues addressed: Semantically monolithic Sigil produces bulky code even
      when the source is later divided mechanically.

      Consequences: Component boundaries determine cohesive modules, while entrypoint
      files remain assembly surfaces.
    }

ImplementationOwnershipWorkflow {
      Decision: Support forward and reconciliation ownership links through
      implementation comments that point to implementation-related section
      occurrences on governing Sigil components and concepts.

      Scope: Governs skill-authored ownership comments in source code, tests, and
      agent-facing workflow Markdown; selectors include interface, state, logic,
      constraints, or cases and exclude goal, decisions, annotations inside Sigil,
      JSON, ordinary documentation, non-text artifacts, and unreviewed reconciliation
      mutation.

      Trade-offs: Implementation-local occurrence links keep governing behavior
      precise beside changing entrypoints, but agents must follow language-specific
      comment syntax and reconcile selectors when Sigil occurrences move.

      Design issues addressed: Ownership links otherwise depend on manual follow-up,
      can become detached from their symbols, or can overstate coverage by naming a
      whole component or concept without its related sections.

      Consequences: Generated entrypoints carry links to exact implementation-related
      contract occurrences, existing repositories gain a controlled reconciliation
      path, and Sigil source remains free of implementation annotations.

      Revisit when: A supported language cannot provide stable comment-to-entrypoint
      association, section occurrences are insufficiently precise, or another
      artifact format needs annotation support.
    }

ImplementationGovernance {
      Decision: Require Sigil coverage inspection before every implementation
      mutation.

      Scope: Governs all implementation artifacts in a Sigil-governed workspace
      regardless of language, format, subsystem, or artifact classification; excludes
      read-only inspection and exact restorative rollback.

      Trade-offs: Every implementation mutation incurs contract-discovery overhead
      before editing, even when preflight later determines that no Sigil change is
      required.

      Design issues addressed: Agents may otherwise classify tests, configuration,
      workflow documents, metadata, validators, or other non-source artifacts as
      outside implementation and bypass Sigil-first development.

      Discarded alternatives: Deciding whether an edit changes behavior before
      preflight preserves the same subjective loophole that caused the gate bypass.

      Consequences: Artifact classification cannot authorize mutation; implementation
      readiness follows inspected coverage and ReviewGate readiness for the exact
      implementation scope.

      Revisit when: The host can deterministically map every repository mutation to
      governing Sigil and enforce the approval states.
    }

ImplementationAlignment {
      Decision: Reconcile completed implementation in a compile-and-alignment loop
      until no drift remains, routing findings by whether they affect a governing
      Sigil contract.

      Scope: Governs post-implementation reconciliation for every approved
      implementation scope; excludes new semantic authoring policy and unrelated
      workspace validation.

      Trade-offs: Completion takes an additional evidence pass and may require a user
      decision after code is written.

      Design issues addressed: A green design compile or passing implementation
      verification alone can leave implementation ownership or contract behavior
      drifted.

      Consequences: Implementation-only ambiguity is resolved with the user inside
      the loop, while contract drift restarts written-Sigil design validation before
      coding resumes.

      Revisit when: The compiler can prove complete implementation alignment without
      host reconciliation evidence.
    }
  }

  cases {
ImplementationCoverage {
      A stable Promise-like caller API can be a component, a material retry algorithm
      can be an expand, and a trivial formatter can be omitted.

      A generated namespace-assembly entrypoint exposes the approved public modules
      while behavior and state remain in their owning implementation units.
    }

ImplementationOwnershipWorkflow {
      A new TypeScript function with one ownership link receives one adjacent line
      comment during approved implementation.

      One entrypoint with multiple ownership links receives one multiline comment
      containing each annotation.

      A component-scoped link can implement logic and constraints occurrences without
      naming a concept.

      A concept-scoped link can use the interface occurrence of that concept.

      An agent-facing Markdown workflow uses an HTML comment and resolves as a
      file-level target.

      A brownfield reconciliation scans relevant Sigil, code, tests, and workflow
      instructions and proposes only implementation comments supported by inspected
      evidence.

      JSON remains unchanged during ownership annotation.
    }

ImplementationGovernance {
      A request to modify any implementation artifact triggers Sigil and coverage
      inspection before the first edit.

      Configuration, tests, validators, metadata, workflow documentation, and
      generated artifacts receive the same preflight as source code.

      A high-level request to fix or build something does not make ReviewGate ready
      for implementation.

      A mechanical edit may omit new Sigil only after preflight establishes complete
      coverage and no material decision.

      Passing tests or validation after an implementation-first edit does not
      legitimize the bypass.

      An agent that detects its own unapproved mutations reports them and performs
      only the user-requested exact rollback before returning to written-Sigil design
      work and implementation preflight.
    }

ImplementationAlignment {
      An implementation that passes tests but disagrees with its ownership map enters
      reconciliation rather than being reported complete.

      A post-implementation question about a local implementation choice is resolved
      with the user and then returns to alignment when the governing contract is
      unchanged.

      A discovered missing observable behavior updates scoped Sigil, runs the design
      compile loop, and then resumes implementation preflight.

      Implementation is complete only after the terminal alignment evidence shows no
      unresolved drift.
    }
  }
}
